  "query_preview": "..Moisture continues to increase across Arizona tonight \nas a favorable flow pattern persists along the New Mexico border. \nThere are two mechanisms for moisture advection at this point, the \ndeep and layered southerly-southwesterly flow and outflow boundaries \nthat drive westward each evening. This will bring another active day \nfor Tuesday east of the I-17 corridor, with some storms creeping as \nfar west as Flagstaff/Payson late in the day. This pattern repeats \nWednesday and may even make another push westward, although drier \nwesterly flow aloft is developing at the same time.\n\nBy Thursday and Friday, the ridge collapses a bit and steering flow \npushes some of the moisture pool temporarily south of our CWA, with \nmaybe the only activity over the White Mountains.\n\nBy Saturday and Sunday, the ridge rapidly rebounds, strengthens and \nthen moves into a very favorable flow pattern for the North American \nMonsoon. This is starting to look like our official start, as next \nweek is looking robust.\n\nAs the moisture pool wobbles in and out of the CWA, we expect very \nwarm temperatures to continue, possibly not peaking until Saturday. \nThe Extreme Heat Warning for the Grand Canyon has been extended \nthrough this period, other lower elevations will experience similar \nconditions.\n\nThat is about it, moisture teases us for a few days and then, it \nsurely looks like the Monsoon begins. Stay tuned!",
